    "updated_at": "2026-04-26 10:06:14",
    "episode_evidence": "Matthew Bloomfield, Partner at Palmer Square Capital Management, highlighted the nuances of the structured credit market, particularly focusing on the CLO market and private credit market. He emphasized the importance of understanding the risks and rewards associated with different types of loans, stating, 'larger borrowers tend to do better in times of tougher macro environments' and that they 'attract better management teams, tend to have better access to capital markets.' This insight underscores the firm's strategy to focus on large borrowers with substantial EBITDA, which they believe are more resilient during market stress. Bloomfield also pointed out the discrepancy in market valuations, noting that 'our public BDC, PSBD, does own the majority of the underlying collateral in broadly syndicated loans' yet trades at a discount compared to BDCs owning mostly private credit, which he suggests might be overvalued given the perceived risk. He further explained the impact of market stress on asset classes, mentioning that 'spreads have just been very, very tight on a historical basis across all of credit,' indicating that current spreads do not adequately compensate for the risks in the environment, leading to recent spread widening.",
    "falsification_tracks": [
      "If the percentage of Palmer Square's BDC's loans to software companies increases significantly and defaults rise, the thesis of a lower risk from disintermediation would be challenged.",
      "Should market data consistently show that syndicated loans are not trading at a discount compared to private credit loans, it would suggest a different market assessment of risk.",
      "If there is a significant and sustained recovery in the valuation of BDCs holding syndicated loans, it could indicate a change in market perception regarding the risk profile of these assets.",
      "A continued increase in the default rates of syndicated loans without a corresponding rise in private credit defaults would undermine the argument for the relative safety of syndicated loans."
    ],
    "insight_title": "\u201cOverblown\u201d Sell-off in Software Loans | Matthew Bloomfield on Public BDCs (Business Development Companies) and Collateralized Loan Obligations (CLOs)",
