    "updated_at": "2026-04-28 10:05:54",
    "episode_evidence": "The groundbreaking field of epigenetic reprogramming discussed in the podcast is poised to revolutionize longevity and health. Dr. David Sinclair, a leading figure in this field, explains that they have identified a subset of Yamanaka genes, referred to as OSK, which have shown potential in reversing aging in various tissues and organs. 'We have a drug candidate that came out of my lab... and we reported... certain genes and combinations that could safely reverse aging,' Sinclair stated. The upcoming human trial will test these genes in curing blindness, building on successful trials in mice and monkeys. 'We find that every tissue that we go into as a field, we see benefits,' Sinclair mentioned, highlighting the broad implications for age reversal across different systems. The technology's potential is underscored by its ability to work throughout the entire body, not just in one cell type, which Sinclair refers to as a 'true longevity therapeutic.'",
    "falsification_tracks": [
      "If the first human trial for epigenetic reprogramming fails to show any positive effects on blindness, it would significantly reduce the conviction in the technology's broader applications.",
      "Should subsequent trials in other tissues and organs not demonstrate age reversal or improvement in function, the thesis of epigenetic reprogramming as a longevity solution would be weakened.",
      "Failure to replicate the findings in independent labs or the emergence of significant safety concerns could halt the progress of this field."
    ],
